diff -Nru '--exclude=build' -x '*.o' pristine/doc/foot.ini.5.scd foot-src/doc/foot.ini.5.scd
--- pristine/doc/foot.ini.5.scd	2026-09-17 12:32:08.927252550 +0200
+++ foot-src/doc/foot.ini.5.scd	2026-09-17 12:38:16.612737104 +0200
@@ -861,6 +861,81 @@
 	string. This option is ignored if
 	*indicator-position=none*. Default: _empty string_.
 
+# SECTION: session
+
+This section only applies when running in server mode (see *-s*,
+*--server* in *foot*(1)). It controls whether, and how, open
+terminal windows are saved to disk and automatically re-opened the
+next time the server starts - for example after a reboot.
+
+Each restored window is re-created with its original working
+directory and size, and shows the last on-screen content (if any
+was saved) followed by a banner. It then re-runs the programs that
+were running in it, as configured in the *session-restore* section,
+and finally executes the command line the window was originally
+launched with (e.g. *footclient lf*), or the shell.
+
+*save*
+	Whether to save, and restore, terminal sessions. Default: _yes_.
+
+*save-interval*
+	How often, in seconds, to save the current session while the
+	server is running, in addition to always saving on a clean
+	shutdown. Default: _20_.
+
+*scrollback-lines*
+	How many additional lines of scrollback, on top of the visible
+	screen, to save for each window. Default: _1000_.
+
+*relaunch-command*
+	Whether to re-launch programs that were running in a window on
+	restore, according to the *session-restore* section. Programs
+	without a rule there are never re-launched. Default: _yes_.
+
+# SECTION: session-restore
+
+Rules for re-launching programs in restored windows. Each key is
+the name of a program (the basename of its executable), and the
+value is the command line to run in its place on restore.
+
+At save time, foot follows the chain of interactive programs
+running on each window's pty, starting at foot's own child (the
+shell, or whatever *footclient* was told to run) and descending
+through programs whose standard input and output are the terminal -
+so a program started from a file manager is found, while helpers
+spawned with pipes are not. Every program with a matching rule is
+re-run on restore, innermost first, each in the directory it was
+running in, before the window's original launch command is
+executed. Quitting a restored program thus lands you where it did
+before.
+
+The value is expanded by */bin/sh* while the program is still
+running, with:
+
+- _$0_ and _"$@"_ set to the program's own command line
+- _$FOOT_PID_ set to the program's PID
+- _$FOOT_CWD_ set to the program's current working directory
+
+This is what lets a rule pick up state that only exists while the
+program is alive, and turn a plain re-launch into a resume. A rule
+that expands to nothing (or fails) means the program is not
+re-launched. Rules are evaluated once per program instance and
+cached.
+
+Example:
+
+```
+[session-restore]
+lf=lf "$@"
+nvim=nvim "$@"
+claude=claude --resume "$(jq -r .sessionId "${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/sessions/$FOOT_PID.json")"
+```
+
+Note that a rule replaces the program's command line entirely; use
+_"$@"_ to keep its original arguments. Rules also apply to the
+program foot's own child is running, so a window launched as
+*footclient claude* is resumed rather than restarted.
+
 # SECTION: url
 
 Note that you can also add custom regular expressions, see the 'regex'
